From: ra80v2-nss project
Subject: [PATCH] wifi: ath11k: back ring skbs with alloc_skb to prevent 32KB page-frag pinning

ath11k's RXDMA monitor/status refill rings (ath11k_dp_rxbufs_replenish), Copy
Engine Rx rings (ath11k_ce_rx_post_pipe), and direct buffer rings allocate
long-lived ring buffers via dev_alloc_skb(), which carves ~2 KB fragments out
of the kernel's shared per-CPU 32 KB (order-3) page_frag_cache.

Under sustained traffic, long-lived ring buffers interleave with short-lived
traffic skbs inside the same 32 KB compound pages. A single unreleased ring
buffer pins its entire 32 KB physical page even after all neighbouring traffic
skbs are freed, causing 5x-10x RAM amplification and eventual OOM reboots on
256 MB IPQ5018 boards (see eisbaw/ath11k-page-frag-fix and qca-nss-drv patch
0105).

Redirect dev_alloc_skb() inside ath11k to alloc_skb(len + NET_SKB_PAD,
GFP_ATOMIC) + skb_reserve(skb, NET_SKB_PAD), preserving identical headroom and
alignment semantics while backing each ring buffer with an independent slab
object that returns memory immediately upon free.

Signed-off-by: ra80v2-nss
---
--- a/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath11k/core.h
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath11k/core.h
@@ -1438,4 +1438,18 @@ static inline u32 ath11k_nfcal_survey_in_use(struct ath11k *ar,
 #undef SURVEY_INFO_IN_USE
 #define SURVEY_INFO_IN_USE ath11k_nfcal_survey_in_use(ar, survey)
 
+#include <linux/skbuff.h>
+
+static inline struct sk_buff *ath11k_alloc_ring_skb(unsigned int length)
+{
+	struct sk_buff *skb = alloc_skb(length + NET_SKB_PAD, GFP_ATOMIC);
+
+	if (likely(skb))
+		skb_reserve(skb, NET_SKB_PAD);
+	return skb;
+}
+
+#undef dev_alloc_skb
+#define dev_alloc_skb(len) ath11k_alloc_ring_skb(len)
+
 #endif /* _CORE_H_ */
